# A stop code is not a stop, and a number in a name is not a platform.
#
# Two traps that have both bitten this app, and both are about reading more
# into the data than it says.
#
# `3034` is one stop code over five stops: BILLINGS BRIDGE 3B, 3C, 4B, 4D and
# BILLINGS BRIDGE / BANK. Four carry a platform, the fifth carries none. A
# search for the code has to offer all five, because the code does not say
# which platform you are standing on.
#
# `CANTERBURY / AD. 860` has no platform at all. The 860 is a municipal address
# in the stop's name. An implementation that parsed a platform out of the name
# would draw one here, and would be confidently wrong on 5,644 of the 5,859
# stops in the real feed, which carry no platform either.
cache ../cache.db
date 2026-09-11
time 08:00
offset -04:00
size 100 14

# One code, five platforms. Typing goes straight to the filter from here, so
# there is no key to press first.
type 3034

# Down one, to BILLINGS BRIDGE 3B, which does carry a platform. Its board says
# so. Read it against the last frame, where the number is part of the name.
down
enter

# esc from a board reached by search lands on the first screen, not back in the
# results: the board's parent is the stop, and a search is not a level.
esc

# A number that is an address, not a platform.
type canterbury
enter
